1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,299
Picture a video as a stack of transparent sheets, one sheet per frame.

2
00:00:04,299 --> 00:00:08,353
A Jewel is a soft three-dimensional blob placed inside that stack.

3
00:00:08,353 --> 00:00:12,161
Its three directions are left-to-right, up-and-down, and time.

4
00:00:12,161 --> 00:00:19,225
Mathematicians call its smooth bell-shaped fade a Gaussian: it is strongest at the center and gradually fades away.

5
00:00:19,225 --> 00:00:22,480
A visible frame is simply one slice through the blob.

6
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:29,634
This is a real fitted video of a singer at a microphone, built from six thousand four hundred seventy-one Jewels.

7
00:00:29,634 --> 00:00:37,003
We mark four strong, moving Jewels, fade away every other contribution, and follow those same four through all sixty-four frames.

8
00:00:37,003 --> 00:00:41,229
Their centers move and their visible cross-sections change as time passes.

9
00:00:41,229 --> 00:00:49,340
We brighten the isolated view equally so the small contributions remain easy to see; their fitted positions, shapes, and colors are unchanged.

10
00:00:50,040 --> 00:00:51,657
A Jewel stores five things.

11
00:00:51,657 --> 00:00:54,053
Its center says where and when it lives.

12
00:00:54,053 --> 00:00:57,227
Its shape says how wide it is and which way it tilts.

13
00:00:57,227 --> 00:01:00,401
Its base color says what it looks like at the center.

14
00:01:00,401 --> 00:01:03,696
A color-change table says how that color varies nearby.

15
00:01:03,696 --> 00:01:06,990
Finally, a strength value says how much it contributes.

16
00:01:06,990 --> 00:01:10,164
The technical name for the shape table is covariance.

17
00:01:10,164 --> 00:01:12,440
Together these use twenty-two numbers.

18
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:17,415
Imagine pushing a cucumber through that stack of frame sheets at an angle.

19
00:01:17,415 --> 00:01:23,365
Each sheet cuts the cucumber at a slightly different horizontal position, so the slice appears to move.

20
00:01:23,365 --> 00:01:25,329
A tilted Jewel works the same way.

21
00:01:25,329 --> 00:01:28,159
If it stretches mostly through time, it persists.

22
00:01:28,159 --> 00:01:33,185
If it tilts across both space and time, its visible position moves from frame to frame.

23
00:01:33,185 --> 00:01:35,380
Motion is built into the shape itself.

24
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:38,691
The base color describes the exact center.

25
00:01:38,691 --> 00:01:45,716
A small three-by-three table describes how red, green, and blue change when we move left, up, or forward in time.

26
00:01:45,716 --> 00:01:48,078
That table is called a color Jacobian.

27
00:01:48,078 --> 00:01:51,746
We will use the full name instead of unexplained shorthand.

28
00:01:51,746 --> 00:01:56,160
Think of it as three tiny color slopes, including a slope through time.

29
00:01:56,860 --> 00:02:00,796
At each pixel, every nearby Jewel paints a small amount of color.

30
00:02:00,796 --> 00:02:05,458
Strong, close Jewels contribute more; weak or distant Jewels contribute less.

31
00:02:05,458 --> 00:02:09,334
We simply add those contributions to a learned background color.

32
00:02:09,334 --> 00:02:15,692
This is called an additive renderer because the contributions are added, like overlapping pools of light.

33
00:02:15,692 --> 00:02:18,780
No Jewel must win exclusive ownership of the pixel.

34
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:24,962
A Jewel fades forever in theory, but after five of its own widths the remaining effect is tiny.

35
00:02:24,962 --> 00:02:27,905
We call the region where it can matter its support.

36
00:02:27,905 --> 00:02:34,138
The renderer divides the volume into boxes so it can quickly find every Jewel whose support reaches a pixel.

37
00:02:34,138 --> 00:02:41,640
This is a filing system, not a placement grid: Jewel centers remain irregular, and long tilted shapes are not accidentally missed.
